# Coding Standards

## General Principles

- **SOLID**: Single responsibility, open/closed, Liskov substitution, interface segregation, dependency inversion
- **DRY**: Extract reusable logic — never duplicate business logic
- **KISS**: Prefer clarity over cleverness — the next developer is you at 3am during the hackathon
- **YAGNI**: Do not build what is not in the current story

## Naming

- Variables and functions: `camelCase`
- Classes, types, interfaces: `PascalCase`
- Constants: `SCREAMING_SNAKE_CASE`
- Files: `kebab-case.ts`
- Boolean variables: prefix with `is`, `has`, `can`, `should`
- Event handlers: prefix with `on` or `handle`

## Functions

- Max 20 lines — extract if longer
- Single responsibility — one function does one thing
- Prefer pure functions — avoid hidden side effects
- Return early on guard conditions (avoid deep nesting)

## Error Handling

- Never silently swallow errors — always log or rethrow
- Use typed/custom error classes for domain errors
- Log errors with context: what happened, where, why
- Surface user-facing errors as meaningful messages — never expose stack traces

## Security (OWASP Essentials)

- **Never** hardcode secrets, tokens, or credentials — use environment variables
- Validate and sanitize all user input at system boundaries
- Never expose internal error details to end users
- Use parameterized queries — no string concatenation in DB queries
- Set appropriate auth headers, CORS policy, and CSP

## Testing

- Write tests for every acceptance criterion
- Unit test pure functions and business logic
- Integration test API endpoints
- Name tests: `should [expected behavior] when [condition]`
- Tests must pass before marking a task checkbox `[x]`

## Code Review Checklist (before marking Ready for Review)

- [ ] Meets all acceptance criteria in the story
- [ ] Tests written and passing
- [ ] No hardcoded secrets or credentials
- [ ] Input validation in place
- [ ] No console.log left in production code
- [ ] File List in story is updated
